  "dom_id": "step-q122318",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14203245/assets/42d1bf23b4ce679748fb1c1765d568b0b32128155f3d6e7770a1b894b0888ded.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{LM}$ in terms of $\\theta .$",
  "worked_solution": "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{MN}$ opposite the given angle $m∠L$  and wish to find the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{LM}.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the hypotenuse is *sine*. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(m∠L) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{6}{LM} \\\\ LM\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =6 \\\\ LM & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
